Package: mdnote
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: qt6-wayland
Description: Yakuake-style sliding Markdown notebook
 mdnote is a quick-access, slide-down Markdown note-taking application
 with a WYSIWYG rich-text editor, live theme switching, an in-editor
 search bar, and a directory-based note sidebar. Notes are stored as
 plain .md files and exported directly to GFM Markdown with no
 external converter required.
 .
 Toggled via a global F10 shortcut: KGlobalAccel handles this natively
 on Plasma. Under GNOME/Wayland, install the gnome-shell-extension-
 mdnote-quake package for the same F10 toggle -- without it (or on any
 other desktop) the window can still be opened via its .desktop entry,
 just not slid in/out on a hotkey.

Package: gnome-shell-extension-mdnote-quake
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, gnome-shell (>= 45)
Recommends: mdnote
Description: GNOME Shell extension for mdnote's Quake-style toggle
 Pins the mdnote sliding notebook to a screen edge with a Yakuake-style
 dropdown animation under GNOME/Wayland, and binds F10 to toggle it --
 working around two things GNOME/mutter doesn't give ordinary Wayland
 clients: forcing window position/size, and true always-on-top.
 .
 Installed system-wide (into /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/); each
 user who wants it still needs to enable it once:
 .
   gnome-extensions enable mdnote-quake@localhost
 .
 GNOME Shell only picks up newly installed/enabled extensions after a
 full session restart (log out and back in under Wayland; Alt+F2, r,
 Enter under X11).
